1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,600
Floating cities challenging hurricane winds

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:07,000
and mountainous waves on the energy frontier.

3
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:11,540
An oil-hungry world awaits as genius and hubris collide.

4
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:15,040
Now, offshore oil drilling on Modern Marbles.

5
00:00:15,041 --> 00:00:29,041
The history of drilling for oil offshore is full of booms,

6
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:33,900
busts, boondoggles, and bankruptcies.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:35,900
As far back as anyone can remember,

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
the sea provoked the passions of risk takers,

9
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:41,600
those who wanted to make a difference.

10
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:43,800
Offshore oil has never been for the faint of heart.

11
00:00:43,801 --> 00:00:47,100
of hard offshore oil has always had this sense of adventure.

12
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:50,000
And there's a real keen edge that puts on it.

13
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:52,700
From giant multinational corporations

14
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:55,480
to independent wildcatters, the oceans

15
00:00:55,580 --> 00:00:58,020
became the ultimate frontier.

16
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:00,280
We've had a history of having people

17
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:03,620
that don't mind thinking broadly and outside the box,

18
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:04,780
so to speak.

19
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:06,560
There were no rules, no regulations.

20
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:09,420
Just go out and do the best you can and hope it works.

21
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:11,520
Fueled by the world's growing demand

22
00:01:11,521 --> 00:01:15,220
dependence on cheap oil, the pioneers pushed farther and

23
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:19,660
farther offshore into remote and hostile environments.

24
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:22,100
Some people compare what we're doing in deep water today

25
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:25,640
to what people are doing in outer space.

26
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:27,740
The oceans and their vast reserves

27
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:31,340
still embody the greatest challenge and reward

28
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:36,180
for those brave or cavalier enough to explore them.

29
00:01:36,280 --> 00:01:38,840
One of the striking things is the extent to which

30
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:40,780
this is a fraternity.

31
00:01:40,781 --> 00:01:43,780
It's people who have literally grown up

32
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:46,220
and gone out deeper and deeper together.

33
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:50,120
The history of offshore oil is brief, yet bountiful.

34
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:53,060
Lots of crazy notions turn into brilliant ideas

35
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:57,260
that materialize in mythic proportions.

36
00:01:57,360 --> 00:02:00,140
The pioneering spirit of drilling for oil offshore

37
00:02:00,240 --> 00:02:04,800
began 24 hours after World War II ended.

38
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:07,300
The rationing of gasoline was lifted,

39
00:02:07,301 --> 00:02:10,940
and America celebrated the wonders of oil.

40
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:13,340
America was ready to roll after World War II.

41
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:16,940
People had been waiting 10, 15, 20 years of depression.

42
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:18,840
They'd been fighting wars for four and five years

43
00:02:18,940 --> 00:02:20,040
away from home.

44
00:02:20,140 --> 00:02:22,540
They wanted to come back and get on with their real lives.

45
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:26,960
And the oil industry had grown steadily from the 1880s forward,

46
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:29,960
but it was really primed to boom after World War II

47
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:34,360
with the growth in highways, the urge to get out and travel.

48
00:02:34,361 --> 00:02:38,600
After the war, demand for oil far exceeded supply.

49
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:41,160
Drivers took to the roads at such a rate

50
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:45,000
that gasoline sales nearly doubled by 1950.

51
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:48,480
By now, oil had exceeded coal in meeting America's energy

52
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:49,880
needs.

53
00:02:49,980 --> 00:02:53,480
It's the wildcatter idea to go find oil.

54
00:02:53,580 --> 00:02:57,020
It's very obvious that bigger fields can be found offshore

55
00:02:57,120 --> 00:03:00,120
than on the land onshore that's been explored so thoroughly.

56
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:01,480
So that's a big attraction.

57
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:03,620
Where do you find some bigger fields?

58
00:03:03,621 --> 00:03:06,720
For millions of years, everything from dinosaurs

59
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:09,920
to dead plants have accumulated on the seafloor.

60
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:13,500
Through time, heat and pressure turned this organic material

61
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:16,400
into petroleum, which has been trapped in reservoirs

62
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:18,300
by layers of rock.

63
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:20,400
The Gulf of Mexico was an ideal place

64
00:03:20,500 --> 00:03:23,100
to start drilling offshore because it had a relatively

65
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:25,480
shallow and gently sloping seabed.

66
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:28,240
Many of the most popular areas for oil and gas exploration

67
00:03:28,340 --> 00:03:31,380
today are what we call historic deltas, just like the Mississippi,

68
00:03:31,381 --> 00:03:35,380
where you've got a lot of junk coming out and being deposited.

69
00:03:35,381 --> 00:03:42,380
The Mississippi River basically collects all of the silt and sand and other type of things

70
00:03:42,381 --> 00:03:45,380
from the mid parts of the United States and comes out and dumps it.

71
00:03:45,381 --> 00:03:47,380
And it's been going on for millions of years.

72
00:03:47,381 --> 00:03:54,380
So the sands that we need to be able to ultimately trap oil and gas are there

73
00:03:54,381 --> 00:03:57,380
and have been laid down over the millions of years.

74
00:03:57,381 --> 00:04:02,220
And it's just a rich, very rich environment for hydrocarbons.

75
00:04:03,300 --> 00:04:06,660
At the birth of the Gulf of Mexico offshore industry,

76
00:04:07,660 --> 00:04:12,820
it's clear that the primary constraint on development of these offshore fields

77
00:04:12,821 --> 00:04:15,980
will be how much it costs to drill exploratory wells.

78
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:22,480
The first concept was to actually build some sort of board road out over the marsh or swamp,

79
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:25,660
build a foundation of piling and put the rig on it.

80
00:04:25,661 --> 00:04:27,700
And that was a lot of construction.

81
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:31,800
And so then the idea came, let's dig a canal

82
00:04:31,900 --> 00:04:36,000
and put your rig on a barge and float the barge out there.

83
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:39,400
In fact, barges of all kinds were left over from the war

84
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:42,420
and in great supply when engineers hired by Kerr

85
00:04:42,520 --> 00:04:45,280
McGee converted a surplus Navy landing craft

86
00:04:45,380 --> 00:04:48,220
into a low-tech hybrid drilling rig.

87
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:53,060
In October 1947, 10 and 1 1⁄2 miles off the Louisiana coast,

88
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:54,860
they struck oil.

89
00:04:54,861 --> 00:05:06,740
For about five years, the companies have more success with this system where they have the old converted World War II ships that are half mobile and half permanent.

90
00:05:06,840 --> 00:05:09,380
They build a small platform with a tender, they call it.

91
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:12,860
And the tender holds most of the equipment and most of the men.

92
00:05:12,980 --> 00:05:17,160
And if you don't find oil, you can at least tow the tender vessel somewhere else.

93
00:05:17,161 --> 00:05:25,960
The trouble is, if you have to build a fixed structure every time you want to drill a well, it becomes very expensive.

94
00:05:26,340 --> 00:05:32,200
And the deeper the water, 25 feet would be five times as expensive as building something in 10 feet of water.

95
00:05:32,700 --> 00:05:38,520
In this period, those are just giant economic costs, and if you can't find a way to salvage some of the costs,

96
00:05:39,020 --> 00:05:41,560
you just can't compete with the onshore drillers.

97
00:05:41,561 --> 00:05:43,760
And it's fairly clear in the early 50s

98
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:46,000
that either that question will be resolved

99
00:05:46,100 --> 00:05:48,700
or the offshore business will not grow.

100
00:05:48,800 --> 00:05:53,040
In 1954, a young marine superintendent named Doc Laborde

101
00:05:53,140 --> 00:05:56,840
designed and built his own offshore drilling rig.

102
00:05:56,940 --> 00:05:59,320
His vessel, which was named Mr. Charlie,

103
00:05:59,420 --> 00:06:02,280
drilled its first well in 40 feet of water.

104
00:06:02,380 --> 00:06:06,060
It was one of the earliest mobile offshore drilling units.

105
00:06:06,160 --> 00:06:08,880
Designed to explore for oil and shallow water,

106
00:06:08,881 --> 00:06:11,420
several compartments in the barge were flooded,

107
00:06:11,520 --> 00:06:14,720
causing it to sink and rest on the sea floor,

108
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:18,260
while the workstation on columns remained above water.

109
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:20,520
If oil was discovered, a platform could then

110
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:22,460
be built to produce the oil.

111
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:25,860
If oil was not discovered, Mr. Charlie pumped the water out,

112
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:29,240
packed up, and moved on to the next prospect.

113
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:34,400
Between 1954 and 1958, more than 30 submersibles were built,

114
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:36,500
each one different from the others.

115
00:06:36,501 --> 00:06:40,540
Mr. Charlie continued drilling until it was retired in 1990

116
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:43,000
and turned into a museum.

117
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:45,080
By permanently mounting a drilling rig

118
00:06:45,180 --> 00:06:47,540
on a submersible barge, oil men were

119
00:06:47,640 --> 00:06:50,880
able to move from well to well in a matter of days.

120
00:06:50,980 --> 00:06:53,180
This innovation not only separated

121
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:56,320
the functions of oil exploration from oil production,

122
00:06:56,420 --> 00:06:58,620
but it lowered the cost of exploration, which

123
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:02,120
helped make offshore oil a viable commodity.

124
00:07:02,220 --> 00:07:03,620
At the bottom is money.

125
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:05,400
Control of the land meant money.

126
00:07:05,401 --> 00:07:09,000
And they didn't have much understanding at all

127
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:12,300
in the early 50s how much money, but it was clear there was

128
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:14,440
money to be made by leasing the land.

129
00:07:14,540 --> 00:07:18,140
In 1953, Congress passed the Submerged Land Act,

130
00:07:18,240 --> 00:07:21,080
which divided continental waters in two.

131
00:07:21,180 --> 00:07:23,080
The area within a three-mile limit

132
00:07:23,180 --> 00:07:25,160
was given to the states and the rest

133
00:07:25,260 --> 00:07:26,980
given to the federal government.

134
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:29,020
This piece of legislation generated

135
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:31,060
enormous revenues for the government,

136
00:07:31,160 --> 00:07:34,020
second only to income taxes.

137
00:07:34,021 --> 00:07:36,020
Offshore oil exploded.

138
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:40,660
By 1957, there were over 100 new mobile drilling units.

139
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:43,260
If offshore oil was going to continue to remain

140
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:45,560
competitive with onshore oil, they

141
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:48,200
would have to find ways to be more efficient and cost

142
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:49,140
effective.

143
00:07:49,240 --> 00:07:53,140
The solution was the lighter and less expensive jackup rig.

144
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:58,580
The jackup is basically a floating barge with legs

145
00:07:58,680 --> 00:08:00,680
that are attached to the barge.

146
00:08:00,780 --> 00:08:02,480
So when you get to your drilling location,

147
00:08:02,481 --> 00:08:06,220
you jack the legs down to the ocean floor

148
00:08:06,320 --> 00:08:08,460
and then raise the barge up out of the water.

149
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:10,960
And that way you have a stable drilling platform

150
00:08:11,060 --> 00:08:13,120
in which to conduct your drilling operations.

151
00:08:13,220 --> 00:08:14,860
The innovative jackup rig

152
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:18,300
attracted the attention of a young oil man from Midland,

153
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:20,000
Texas.

154
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:23,000
We worked out a deal with Letourneau,

155
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:27,200
who had a new kind of offshore drilling rig, three-legged rig.

156
00:08:27,300 --> 00:08:29,640
None of the major companies or drilling contractors

157
00:08:29,740 --> 00:08:30,640
would touch it.

158
00:08:30,641 --> 00:08:35,480
So we kind of gambled and built the first and second and third.

159
00:08:35,580 --> 00:08:37,880
With little or no solid information

160
00:08:37,980 --> 00:08:40,780
about wind and wave strength in the Gulf,

161
00:08:40,880 --> 00:08:44,620
the prospectors and their rigs were vulnerable to disaster.

162
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:46,660
Our timing was good.

163
00:08:46,760 --> 00:08:48,660
We suffered a couple of setbacks.

164
00:08:48,760 --> 00:08:52,160
The third one or fourth one disappeared in a hurricane.

165
00:08:52,260 --> 00:08:53,600
I mean, it just vanished.

166
00:08:53,700 --> 00:08:56,400
We'd taken the people off, and it was gone.

167
00:08:56,500 --> 00:08:58,400
$6 million investment.

168
00:08:58,401 --> 00:09:01,940
Currently, operators and contractors use jackups

169
00:09:02,040 --> 00:09:04,840
to drill most offshore wells.

170
00:09:04,940 --> 00:09:06,880
Apart from the legs and the jacks,

171
00:09:06,980 --> 00:09:10,780
the platform resembles that of a standard drilling barge.

172
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:13,420
Through the years, different jackup designs

173
00:09:13,520 --> 00:09:15,960
have increased their water depth from 80 feet

174
00:09:16,060 --> 00:09:18,580
to almost 400 feet.

175
00:09:18,680 --> 00:09:21,360
It's an amazing creative era for that mobile drilling

176
00:09:21,460 --> 00:09:24,620
technology, and the reason it's so important.

177
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:26,700
So a lot of people have an incentive

178
00:09:26,701 --> 00:09:30,400
to work hard on mobility so that you can find the oil

179
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:32,940
and move on and then have a permanent platform built

180
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:34,140
wherever you find it.

181
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:36,440
We had some ups and downs, but I liked it.

182
00:09:36,540 --> 00:09:37,640
I loved the business.

183
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:38,740
It was pioneering.

184
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:44,460
There are currently nearly 400 jackup rigs prowling

185
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:46,460
the world's oceans in search of new oil.

186
00:09:50,100 --> 00:09:52,760
It's been said that a drilling rig is nothing more

187
00:09:52,860 --> 00:09:55,300
than a portable hole factory.

188
00:09:55,301 --> 00:09:58,340
be it large or small, offshore or on,

189
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:01,540
its main job is drilling wells.

190
00:10:01,640 --> 00:10:04,680
In 1883, spiritualist H.L. Williams

191
00:10:04,780 --> 00:10:07,140
didn't even need to drill a hole to find oil

192
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:09,880
after a modest earthquake sent a spurt of the stuff

193
00:10:09,980 --> 00:10:12,820
as large as a man's arm right out of the ground

194
00:10:12,920 --> 00:10:16,880
near his home several miles southeast of Santa Barbara.

195
00:10:16,980 --> 00:10:20,360
Eventually, Williams and others started drilling plenty of holes.

196
00:10:20,460 --> 00:10:23,700
And by 1895, there were 28 oil wells

197
00:10:23,701 --> 00:10:28,800
producing 16,904 barrels a year.

198
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:31,640
When better producing sites were found on the beach,

199
00:10:31,740 --> 00:10:33,940
Williams built a rickety wooden wharf

200
00:10:34,040 --> 00:10:37,040
and drilled the first wells over the sea.

201
00:10:37,140 --> 00:10:39,220
By the turn of the century, piers and wharfs

202
00:10:39,320 --> 00:10:41,820
reached out 1,200 feet from the shoreline

203
00:10:41,920 --> 00:10:43,920
into water 30 feet deep.

204
00:10:44,020 --> 00:10:47,120
By 1910, all of these fields were in sharp decline,

205
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:50,060
and drilling for oil off piers into the Pacific Ocean

206
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:53,700
soon ran into a geological and technical wall.

207
00:10:53,701 --> 00:10:56,440
The continental shelf off the coast of California

208
00:10:56,540 --> 00:10:59,840
was steep and deep and loaded with fault lines.

209
00:10:59,940 --> 00:11:02,280
Drilling techniques used in the Gulf of Mexico

210
00:11:02,380 --> 00:11:03,940
were impractical.

211
00:11:04,040 --> 00:11:06,840
If they wanted any more oil out of this narrow channel,

212
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:08,980
they would have to leave land and drill deeper

213
00:11:09,080 --> 00:11:10,880
than they'd ever drilled before.

214
00:11:10,980 --> 00:11:13,380
The solution would come 50 years later

215
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:16,720
in an unusual scientific experiment.

216
00:11:16,820 --> 00:11:19,960
There was a glorious age for science in the late 50s

217
00:11:20,060 --> 00:11:21,120
and early 60s.

218
00:11:21,220 --> 00:11:22,760
We're looking at space adventures.

219
00:11:22,761 --> 00:11:24,720
And they thought of the Moho project

220
00:11:24,820 --> 00:11:27,200
as the exploration of inner space, they called it.

221
00:11:27,300 --> 00:11:29,700
If we were going toward the moon as we thought we were,

222
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:31,800
why not go toward the center of the Earth?

223
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:34,040
Moho is the name given to the area

224
00:11:34,140 --> 00:11:37,140
between the Earth's crust and the Earth's mantle.

225
00:11:37,240 --> 00:11:40,820
While the Earth's crust is approximately 24 miles thick,

226
00:11:40,920 --> 00:11:43,240
it is thinnest deep under the oceans.

227
00:11:43,340 --> 00:11:46,380
Scientists and oil men alike were interested in drilling

228
00:11:46,480 --> 00:11:49,420
this deep to retrieve sediment samples that could tell them

229
00:11:49,520 --> 00:11:51,880
how the Earth was formed as well as how and where

230
00:11:51,881 --> 00:11:54,780
where oil is trapped.

231
00:11:54,880 --> 00:12:01,160
This required, however, particularly large leaps forward

232
00:12:01,260 --> 00:12:04,200
in the technology of drilling offshore.

233
00:12:04,300 --> 00:12:07,560
The project presented an enormous challenge,

234
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:12,100
how to drill to a depth of 25,000 feet at a site covered

235
00:12:12,200 --> 00:12:16,040
by more than 15,000 feet of water, when at the time,

236
00:12:16,140 --> 00:12:21,540
200 feet of water was considered a test of technology.

237
00:12:21,541 --> 00:12:24,040
A mohole drill must go at least a mile deeper

238
00:12:24,140 --> 00:12:26,840
than the deepest hole ever drilled on land.

239
00:12:26,940 --> 00:12:29,140
In 1960, a group of oil companies

240
00:12:29,240 --> 00:12:31,580
converted a surplus yard fighter barge

241
00:12:31,680 --> 00:12:33,880
and called it the CUS-1.

242
00:12:33,980 --> 00:12:36,080
The vessel was designed with an oil derrick

243
00:12:36,180 --> 00:12:39,600
over a hole or moon pool in the middle of the ship.

244
00:12:39,700 --> 00:12:42,700
At this point, engineers faced two major problems

245
00:12:42,800 --> 00:12:45,000
if they were going to drill in deep water.

246
00:12:45,100 --> 00:12:48,740
First, the drilling column would have to be over two miles long.

247
00:12:48,840 --> 00:12:51,040
And second, how to hold the ship steady

248
00:12:51,041 --> 00:12:54,480
waters too deep for anchors.

249
00:12:54,580 --> 00:12:56,240
When you go to floating drilling,

250
00:12:56,340 --> 00:12:58,280
the industry had to come up with ways

251
00:12:58,380 --> 00:13:02,520
to handle the motion of the vessel in relationship

252
00:13:02,620 --> 00:13:05,220
to the ocean floor, which is fixed.

253
00:13:05,320 --> 00:13:09,500
And so over the years, we have innovated and designed

254
00:13:09,600 --> 00:13:11,700
a lot of different equipment that

255
00:13:11,800 --> 00:13:14,460
compensates for this motion, because a ship moves up

256
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:17,000
and down in the water depending upon the waves

257
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:19,000
and the wind that's happening.

258
00:13:19,001 --> 00:13:22,540
Having seen a New England lobster fisherman use his engine

259
00:13:22,541 --> 00:13:26,600
to remain stationary in water while pulling his traps,

260
00:13:26,601 --> 00:13:29,140
engineers decided that several propellers

261
00:13:29,141 --> 00:13:33,040
would be installed on the ship to counteract the wind, waves,

262
00:13:33,041 --> 00:13:35,920
and ocean currents.

263
00:13:35,921 --> 00:13:40,960
In March of 1961, 40 miles off the coast of Baja, California,

264
00:13:40,961 --> 00:13:45,600
in 12-foot seas, the CUS-1 drilled a 110-foot hole

265
00:13:45,601 --> 00:13:48,700
in 11,700 feet of water.

266
00:13:48,701 --> 00:13:52,400
samples dating back 15 to 20 million years,

267
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:55,540
revealed an ocean with far more life than today,

268
00:13:55,640 --> 00:13:57,280
which was good news for the oil men,

269
00:13:57,380 --> 00:14:00,720
because where there are fossils, there is fuel.

270
00:14:00,820 --> 00:14:03,480
As quickly as it started, the Mohol project

271
00:14:03,580 --> 00:14:05,820
ended in bureaucratic bickering.

272
00:14:05,920 --> 00:14:08,680
While the project did not drill to the center of the Earth,

273
00:14:08,780 --> 00:14:12,160
it did put Santa Barbara at the center of offshore oil

274
00:14:12,260 --> 00:14:15,620
drilling, which was remarkable considering the 100-foot depth

275
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:18,200
that was manageable from offshore rigs in the Gulf

276
00:14:18,201 --> 00:14:21,900
at the time, not counting the oil produced off the piers

277
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:23,500
at the turn of the century.

278
00:14:23,600 --> 00:14:27,740
California produced its first offshore oil in 1962.

279
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:30,240
The market for marine drilling took off.

280
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:32,640
After several years of drilling successfully,

281
00:14:32,740 --> 00:14:34,960
ships were developed with larger hulls and bigger

282
00:14:35,060 --> 00:14:36,460
drilling platforms.

283
00:14:36,560 --> 00:14:39,660
Positioning was aided by the use of a taut wire system

284
00:14:39,760 --> 00:14:41,460
attached to the sea floor.

285
00:14:41,560 --> 00:14:44,560
Any change in the angle signaled a series of thrusts

286
00:14:44,660 --> 00:14:48,200
from the propellers to push the vessel back into position.

287
00:14:48,201 --> 00:14:51,400
With drill ships, man could now begin to explore for oil

288
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:53,640
almost anywhere on the planet.

289
00:14:53,740 --> 00:14:57,200
And still, the technology improved.

290
00:14:57,300 --> 00:15:02,340
And at the heart of much of the 1960s forward technology

291
00:15:02,440 --> 00:15:04,620
and offshore, and indeed in the industry as a whole,

292
00:15:04,720 --> 00:15:07,520
is the growing use of sophisticated computers.

293
00:15:07,620 --> 00:15:09,580
At the edge of offshore technology

294
00:15:09,680 --> 00:15:13,400
during the last 40 years is seismic data, which

295
00:15:13,500 --> 00:15:17,500
was directly affected by the computer revolution in the 60s.

296
00:15:17,501 --> 00:15:18,700
You've got to have a picture.

297
00:15:18,800 --> 00:15:21,400
You've got to have some a priori idea of what you're,

298
00:15:21,500 --> 00:15:23,400
what you're, what you're drilling for.

299
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:26,700
And I think that the reason that we've come so far

300
00:15:26,800 --> 00:15:31,220
in a relatively recent amount of time

301
00:15:31,320 --> 00:15:33,480
is that those pictures are getting better and better.

302
00:15:33,580 --> 00:15:36,480
What people are looking for is areas where oil or gas

303
00:15:36,580 --> 00:15:38,380
are likely to be trapped.

304
00:15:38,480 --> 00:15:41,460
In the very early days, they'd toss a stick of dynamite

305
00:15:41,560 --> 00:15:45,160
over the side of a boat, towing sound sensors behind it.

306
00:15:45,161 --> 00:15:47,900
The sensors measured the time it took for the sound of the

307
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,360
explosion to travel to the bottom of the water

308
00:15:50,460 --> 00:15:53,100
and back up to the boat.

309
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:56,180
These sound waves were recorded and made into a picture

310
00:15:56,280 --> 00:15:59,140
or structural map of the subsurface.

311
00:15:59,240 --> 00:16:02,180
In the beginning, all oil men had was a crude picture

312
00:16:02,280 --> 00:16:03,720
with bumps on it.

313
00:16:03,820 --> 00:16:07,980
A lot of the early oil was found on what I like to call

314
00:16:08,080 --> 00:16:09,380
bumpology.

315
00:16:09,480 --> 00:16:12,660
So many of the early discoveries we saw all around the world

316
00:16:12,661 --> 00:16:16,060
or drilling bumps on the surface, if you will.

317
00:16:16,160 --> 00:16:18,400
The bumps on earliest seismic pictures

318
00:16:18,500 --> 00:16:21,140
indicated rock formations on the ocean floor

319
00:16:21,240 --> 00:16:23,900
that could possibly trap oil and gas.

320
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,600
But a lot of those bumps were a bust,

321
00:16:26,700 --> 00:16:29,180
an estimated 1 in 15 produced oil,

322
00:16:29,280 --> 00:16:31,540
which is an expensive drilling ratio.

323
00:16:31,640 --> 00:16:33,040
For years in the Gulf of Mexico,

324
00:16:33,140 --> 00:16:37,120
we worked down to a certain economic basement, if you will.

325
00:16:37,220 --> 00:16:38,520
That's as far as we could see.

326
00:16:38,620 --> 00:16:41,020
Anything beneath that in the subsurface,

327
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:42,660
we didn't pay any attention to, because there's

328
00:16:42,661 --> 00:16:44,320
because we had no idea what was there.

329
00:16:44,321 --> 00:16:48,000
But as sound imaging and computer capabilities improved,

330
00:16:48,001 --> 00:16:52,300
so did the quality of the information in the bumps.

331
00:16:52,301 --> 00:16:55,440
One of the real keys was looking at recording these echoes

332
00:16:55,441 --> 00:16:56,500
in three dimensions.

333
00:16:56,501 --> 00:16:59,500
We call it 3D seismic, where you put sensors

334
00:16:59,501 --> 00:17:01,480
in an aerial distribution.

335
00:17:01,481 --> 00:17:04,540
And you can start to record all these echoes coming in

336
00:17:04,541 --> 00:17:06,680
from various reflections.

337
00:17:06,681 --> 00:17:10,080
And through enormous computer power,

338
00:17:10,081 --> 00:17:12,060
you can reconstruct these images so that they're

339
00:17:12,061 --> 00:17:15,860
an accurate representation of the three-dimensional structure of the Earth.

340
00:17:15,861 --> 00:17:21,520
The very latest seismic technique is the ability to capture subsurface pictures of oil as it

341
00:17:21,521 --> 00:17:23,740
moves through a reservoir.

342
00:17:23,741 --> 00:17:26,360
That's the fourth dimension, it's the time dimension.

343
00:17:26,361 --> 00:17:30,300
The greatest thing about this today is that historically we've been very inefficient in

344
00:17:30,301 --> 00:17:32,160
producing our reservoirs.

345
00:17:32,161 --> 00:17:36,680
Maybe 30 to 35 percent of the oil in place when we discover it can we get out of the

346
00:17:36,681 --> 00:17:42,020
ground, partly because we can't see it, partly because it's not produced properly.

347
00:17:42,021 --> 00:17:44,460
pump too hard here and not hard enough here.

348
00:17:44,461 --> 00:17:49,060
4D hopefully will allow us to push that efficiency up to maybe

349
00:17:49,061 --> 00:17:51,060
50, 60, 70 percent.

350
00:17:51,061 --> 00:17:53,020
So we don't have to find new reserves.

351
00:17:53,021 --> 00:17:55,260
We have to produce the ones we've already found better.

352
00:17:58,140 --> 00:18:01,500
Today, thanks in part to improved seismic information,

353
00:18:01,501 --> 00:18:04,460
an estimated one in four wells drill produces oil.

354
00:18:07,300 --> 00:18:09,380
Nearly three-fourths of the Earth's surface

355
00:18:09,381 --> 00:18:11,100
is covered by oceans.

356
00:18:11,101 --> 00:18:13,500
And nothing embodies the challenge of oil drilling

357
00:18:13,600 --> 00:18:16,000
more than these superstructures.

358
00:18:16,100 --> 00:18:19,000
Sitting defiantly offshore, these floating industrial

359
00:18:19,100 --> 00:18:22,640
cathedrals have revolutionized the search for oil and gas

360
00:18:22,740 --> 00:18:24,840
beneath the ocean floor.

361
00:18:24,940 --> 00:18:28,180
Today's drilling and production platforms in the Gulf of Mexico

362
00:18:28,280 --> 00:18:33,220
are a far cry from the permanent or fixed platforms of the past.

363
00:18:33,320 --> 00:18:36,200
The first structure was in 20 feet of water.

364
00:18:36,300 --> 00:18:38,220
And we thought that was deep.

365
00:18:38,320 --> 00:18:40,060
The earliest platforms were made out

366
00:18:40,061 --> 00:18:43,900
and required considerable bracing, which offered limited resistance

367
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:45,100
to wave action.

368
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:47,700
With the availability of steel after World War II,

369
00:18:47,800 --> 00:18:51,240
the construction of modern platforms began in 1947

370
00:18:51,340 --> 00:18:53,700
with the installation of the first steel structure

371
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:57,280
in 50 feet of water.

372
00:18:57,380 --> 00:19:00,420
It was 19, I think, 54 before a platform

373
00:19:00,520 --> 00:19:03,320
was put in as deep water as 100 feet of water.

374
00:19:03,420 --> 00:19:06,920
And these were fixed structures that actually consist

375
00:19:06,921 --> 00:19:09,720
uh, consists of a framework called a jacket.

376
00:19:09,820 --> 00:19:12,220
A jacket is a tall vertical section

377
00:19:12,320 --> 00:19:16,260
made of tubular steel, which is the foundation of a platform.

378
00:19:16,360 --> 00:19:18,700
Piles driven through the base of the foundation

379
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:20,860
secure it to the sea floor.

380
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:22,900
Additional sections on top of the jacket

381
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,800
provide space for the drilling rig, equipment, and crew.

382
00:19:26,900 --> 00:19:32,340
By 1959, platforms were installed in 200 feet of water.

383
00:19:32,440 --> 00:19:34,420
In the very early days, there was no way

384
00:19:34,421 --> 00:19:37,320
to have any kind of temporary structure.

385
00:19:37,420 --> 00:19:40,020
You had to go out and build a complete platform,

386
00:19:40,120 --> 00:19:41,820
put a rig on it, and drill.

387
00:19:41,920 --> 00:19:44,160
And then you might find oil and you might not.

388
00:19:44,260 --> 00:19:46,560
Historically, the size and development

389
00:19:46,660 --> 00:19:49,000
of fixed production platforms was determined

390
00:19:49,100 --> 00:19:52,100
by the size of the support equipment that built them.

391
00:19:52,200 --> 00:19:54,800
As the lifting capacity of the support equipment grew,

392
00:19:54,900 --> 00:19:58,940
so did the size and weight of the oil production structures.

393
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:02,520
When we began in the very shallow water,

394
00:20:02,521 --> 00:20:05,820
The structures were probably wider than they are tall.

395
00:20:05,920 --> 00:20:07,720
But as we moved out into deeper water,

396
00:20:07,820 --> 00:20:11,220
the structures that would keep the cost within reason,

397
00:20:11,320 --> 00:20:13,160
had to make them thinner.

398
00:20:13,260 --> 00:20:16,260
And so they became more tall and more limber.

399
00:20:16,360 --> 00:20:18,360
Fixed platforms of all sizes are

400
00:20:18,460 --> 00:20:21,980
constructed the same way today as the pioneers first built

401
00:20:22,080 --> 00:20:22,880
them.

402
00:20:22,980 --> 00:20:25,880
The jacket for Bullwinkle, the tallest offshore fixed

403
00:20:25,980 --> 00:20:29,020
platform in the world, was constructed in one piece

404
00:20:29,021 --> 00:20:31,060
in Texas in 1988.

405
00:20:31,160 --> 00:20:34,420
The 77,000-ton jacket was loaded onto a barge

406
00:20:34,520 --> 00:20:38,520
and towed 332 nautical miles to the production site

407
00:20:38,620 --> 00:20:40,560
in the Gulf of Mexico.

408
00:20:40,660 --> 00:20:42,960
Bullwinkle was positioned on site,

409
00:20:43,060 --> 00:20:45,400
and under the watchful eye of nervous engineers,

410
00:20:45,500 --> 00:20:48,040
the structure was launched slowly off the barge,

411
00:20:48,140 --> 00:20:51,540
gradually sinking under its own weight.

412
00:20:51,640 --> 00:20:54,840
90 seconds later, the skyscraper-sized structure

413
00:20:54,940 --> 00:20:57,340
was afloat over the place where it would be attached

414
00:20:57,341 --> 00:20:59,180
to the ocean floor.

415
00:20:59,280 --> 00:21:02,140
Engineers opened flood valves that allowed water

416
00:21:02,240 --> 00:21:04,980
into the lower chambers of the tubular legs,

417
00:21:05,080 --> 00:21:10,280
bringing the massive structure into an upright position.

418
00:21:10,380 --> 00:21:14,300
Today, Bullwinkle stands nearly 300 feet higher

419
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:16,500
than the Sears Tower in Chicago.

420
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:20,300
Drilling in depths of 18,000 feet beneath the ocean floor,

421
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:23,500
this superstructure is capable of tapping oil reserves

422
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:27,040
almost two miles away through an innovative technique

423
00:21:27,041 --> 00:21:29,080
called directional drilling.

424
00:21:29,180 --> 00:21:31,540
With a 60-well capacity, Bullwinkle

425
00:21:31,640 --> 00:21:36,680
produces 200,000 barrels of oil and 306 million cubic feet

426
00:21:36,780 --> 00:21:38,280
of gas a day.

427
00:21:38,380 --> 00:21:40,180
That's the exciting aspect of the thing.

428
00:21:40,280 --> 00:21:44,560
We're doing stuff that people never thought was possible.

429
00:21:44,660 --> 00:21:48,420
Since 1947, more than 6,000 fixed platforms

430
00:21:48,520 --> 00:21:52,740
have been installed offshore in 55 countries around the world.

431
00:21:52,840 --> 00:21:56,100
By 1989, the year after Bullwinkle was installed,

432
00:21:56,101 --> 00:21:59,200
Removal of fixed platforms outpaced installations

433
00:21:59,300 --> 00:22:02,500
in the Gulf of Mexico for the first time in history.

434
00:22:02,600 --> 00:22:06,440
But the next step offshore was already underway.

435
00:22:06,540 --> 00:22:09,040
We're reaching the end of the fixed platform area.

436
00:22:09,140 --> 00:22:11,920
That doesn't mean as oil is found in the 400 and 600

437
00:22:12,020 --> 00:22:14,320
and 1,200 feet of water, the fixed platform

438
00:22:14,420 --> 00:22:16,320
is not going to be the choice.

439
00:22:16,420 --> 00:22:21,100
But the real pioneering now is in the floating drilling.

440
00:22:21,200 --> 00:22:25,500
Located 125 miles east-southeast of New Orleans,

441
00:22:25,501 --> 00:22:29,540
Ram Powell is a $1 billion state-of-the-art floating

442
00:22:29,640 --> 00:22:31,780
drilling and production platform.

443
00:22:31,880 --> 00:22:35,140
In September of 1997, oil began flowing

444
00:22:35,240 --> 00:22:39,820
through its steel veins 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

445
00:22:39,920 --> 00:22:43,620
At the current rate, it will take an estimated 25 to 30 years

446
00:22:43,720 --> 00:22:48,760
to deplete an oil field estimated at 250 million barrels.

447
00:22:48,860 --> 00:22:51,100
Actually, it's a floating structure.

448
00:22:51,200 --> 00:22:54,500
It weighs 41,000 tons.

449
00:22:54,501 --> 00:23:02,100
And this is 467 feet tall, size 46 stories.

450
00:23:02,200 --> 00:23:04,740
Ram Powell is a tension leg platform

451
00:23:04,840 --> 00:23:09,480
or semi-submersible capable of handling as many as 20 wells.

452
00:23:09,580 --> 00:23:12,520
Sitting in water more than 3,000 feet deep,

453
00:23:12,620 --> 00:23:18,160
it is secured to the ocean floor by 12 tendon-like pipes.

454
00:23:18,260 --> 00:23:23,760
I try to tell people that the tendons are like rubber bands.

455
00:23:23,761 --> 00:23:30,360
And the more you stretch them, the more tense they get or the more rigid they get.

456
00:23:30,900 --> 00:23:32,200
So it's the same with pipes.

457
00:23:32,201 --> 00:23:37,040
We are nailed in to the ocean floor, the ends of these pipes,

458
00:23:37,280 --> 00:23:40,060
and they come up and attach to the bottom of the hull,

459
00:23:40,580 --> 00:23:45,680
and it holds us rigid against the environmental forces such as wind and waves.

460
00:23:46,120 --> 00:23:52,440
Designed to withstand hurricane force winds of 140 miles per hour and waves 72 feet high,

461
00:23:52,441 --> 00:23:56,620
The structure moves about 300 feet from side to side.

462
00:23:56,720 --> 00:24:00,920
Ram Powell is capable of pumping 60,000 barrels of oil

463
00:24:01,020 --> 00:24:04,760
and 260 million cubic feet of gas a day.

464
00:24:04,860 --> 00:24:08,320
That's enough oil to drive your car more than 50 million miles

465
00:24:08,420 --> 00:24:11,800
and enough gas to heat 3,000 average homes for a year.

466
00:24:11,900 --> 00:24:14,660
Managing the volume of those natural resources

467
00:24:14,760 --> 00:24:17,600
is what keeps Ram Powell afloat.

468
00:24:17,700 --> 00:24:21,740
We got six wells are already drilled and completed.

469
00:24:21,741 --> 00:24:25,180
and they're anywhere from 18,000 to 25,000 feet.

470
00:24:25,280 --> 00:24:28,080
The oil and gas are separated on the platform

471
00:24:28,180 --> 00:24:31,380
in a maze of pipes and then sent directly to refineries

472
00:24:31,480 --> 00:24:32,580
on shore.

473
00:24:32,680 --> 00:24:34,920
At the rate of 42 barrels a minute,

474
00:24:35,020 --> 00:24:38,320
the oil and gas travel through a network of underwater pipes

475
00:24:38,420 --> 00:24:40,460
100 miles long.

476
00:24:40,560 --> 00:24:43,120
Floating deepwater platforms like Ram Powell

477
00:24:43,220 --> 00:24:46,240
were designed and developed not only to withstand the realities

478
00:24:46,340 --> 00:24:50,160
of wind and waves, but also the harsh economic reality

479
00:24:50,161 --> 00:24:55,160
of increasing demand and lower oil prices in the 80s and 90s.

480
00:24:55,260 --> 00:24:58,060
The industry digs in its heels and realizes

481
00:24:58,160 --> 00:25:00,400
if you can't control the price, you've

482
00:25:00,500 --> 00:25:01,900
got to control your cost.

483
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,400
To cut costs and still remain competitive,

484
00:25:04,500 --> 00:25:08,020
the oil companies had to find safe ways to use technology

485
00:25:08,120 --> 00:25:11,320
to drill more efficiently.

486
00:25:11,420 --> 00:25:13,620
It used to be where you needed three or four people working

487
00:25:13,720 --> 00:25:14,620
on a brick floor.

488
00:25:14,720 --> 00:25:16,220
Now you only need one.

489
00:25:16,320 --> 00:25:18,320
Most of the work is done with a machine.

490
00:25:18,321 --> 00:25:20,780
and the man controlling it in that shack.

491
00:25:20,880 --> 00:25:23,420
You got people working away from the equipment now,

492
00:25:23,520 --> 00:25:27,700
moving the joysticks and pushing buttons.

493
00:25:27,800 --> 00:25:29,740
When drilling moved into deep water,

494
00:25:29,840 --> 00:25:32,560
the jobs once performed by commercial divers

495
00:25:32,660 --> 00:25:39,480
became the tasks of remotely operated vehicles, or ROVs.

496
00:25:39,580 --> 00:25:43,320
Although we're not as flexible as having a human being

497
00:25:43,420 --> 00:25:47,380
on the bottom doing the work, the ROV companies and oil

498
00:25:47,381 --> 00:25:50,380
companies have gotten together, and they've eliminated

499
00:25:50,480 --> 00:25:53,680
those problems by making the platforms and the fields

500
00:25:53,780 --> 00:25:56,660
diver friendly so that the ROV could operate all the valves

501
00:25:56,760 --> 00:26:00,200
and they could do all the tasks that needed to be done.

502
00:26:00,300 --> 00:26:03,000
About the size of a sport utility vehicle,

503
00:26:03,100 --> 00:26:06,300
this ROV is equipped with six cameras,

504
00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:09,300
arms capable of lifting 600 pounds,

505
00:26:09,400 --> 00:26:12,700
and wrists that can turn 360 degrees,

506
00:26:12,800 --> 00:26:15,940
and it never complains about working under pressure.

507
00:26:15,941 --> 00:26:20,640
The pressures at 3,200 feet are about 1,600 pounds

508
00:26:20,740 --> 00:26:23,940
per square inch, which would crush a human being if he was

509
00:26:24,040 --> 00:26:25,580
able to go to that depth.

510
00:26:25,680 --> 00:26:28,320
The dexterity of the vehicle's arms and wrists

511
00:26:28,420 --> 00:26:32,520
are remotely controlled by a master arm above.

512
00:26:32,620 --> 00:26:34,420
We open and close valves.

513
00:26:34,520 --> 00:26:37,220
We can do everything from cutting cables to ropes

514
00:26:37,320 --> 00:26:41,100
to installing hydraulic jumpers and electrical cables.

515
00:26:41,200 --> 00:26:43,540
While technology has automated much

516
00:26:43,541 --> 00:26:46,540
of the work aboard Ram Powell, it has also influenced

517
00:26:46,541 --> 00:26:50,540
the daily life of the 110 permanent employees

518
00:26:50,541 --> 00:26:53,540
who consider this platform home.

519
00:26:53,541 --> 00:26:56,540
They eat, sleep, and work out here almost six months

520
00:26:56,541 --> 00:26:59,540
of the year.

521
00:26:59,541 --> 00:27:03,540
We work 14 days on and 14 days off.

522
00:27:03,541 --> 00:27:08,540
So we come out and work 12-hour shifts,

523
00:27:08,541 --> 00:27:12,540
6 a.m. to 6 p.m. or 6 p.m. to 6 a.m.

524
00:27:12,541 --> 00:27:14,940
because it has to be manned 24 hours a day.

525
00:27:15,040 --> 00:27:17,180
The days go by pretty fast.

526
00:27:17,280 --> 00:27:20,780
In the evening, we tend to relax and kind of read

527
00:27:20,880 --> 00:27:22,080
and talk to the family.

528
00:27:22,180 --> 00:27:23,820
We're allowed to make some phone calls

529
00:27:23,920 --> 00:27:25,480
and keep in touch with them.

530
00:27:25,580 --> 00:27:28,320
Staying in touch with friends and families on shore

531
00:27:28,420 --> 00:27:31,120
is an important part of life out here.

532
00:27:31,220 --> 00:27:32,920
If they can't reach them by phone,

533
00:27:33,020 --> 00:27:34,800
they can use one of several computers

534
00:27:34,900 --> 00:27:36,800
to access the internet.

535
00:27:36,900 --> 00:27:39,640
But like any long-distance relationship,

536
00:27:39,740 --> 00:27:42,040
working offshore takes some getting used to.

537
00:27:42,041 --> 00:27:43,240
We've learned to adapt.

538
00:27:43,340 --> 00:27:46,720
The only thing is that you miss a lot of the family functions,

539
00:27:46,820 --> 00:27:49,180
a lot of the kids, baseball, whatever.

540
00:27:49,280 --> 00:27:50,780
We do miss a lot of that.

541
00:27:50,880 --> 00:27:52,920
The Ram Powell platform is actually

542
00:27:53,020 --> 00:27:56,380
a floating full-service city, generating enough power

543
00:27:56,480 --> 00:27:58,960
to light up 7,000 homes.

544
00:27:59,060 --> 00:28:03,060
It also produces 1,100 gallons of fresh water each day.

545
00:28:03,160 --> 00:28:05,600
But the bottom line out here is safety.

546
00:28:05,700 --> 00:28:09,360
With more than 1 million accident-free hours to their credit,

547
00:28:09,361 --> 00:28:14,000
Employees on Ram Powell are expected to focus on their work.

548
00:28:14,100 --> 00:28:15,960
Rooms are cleaned each day and are

549
00:28:16,060 --> 00:28:17,860
equipped with cable television.

550
00:28:17,960 --> 00:28:19,700
Laundry is done for you.

551
00:28:19,800 --> 00:28:23,600
And more than 125,000 meals are served around the clock

552
00:28:23,700 --> 00:28:24,600
each year.

553
00:28:24,700 --> 00:28:27,580
Recreation varies from educational programs

554
00:28:27,680 --> 00:28:29,580
to increase your skill level and pay

555
00:28:29,680 --> 00:28:32,880
to an exercise room to increase your fitness.

556
00:28:32,980 --> 00:28:35,280
And while they are no match for the pros,

557
00:28:35,380 --> 00:28:39,020
offshore ping pong can get serious.

558
00:28:39,021 --> 00:28:42,460
Rampowl is a reusable mobile platform.

559
00:28:42,560 --> 00:28:44,820
Once it has depleted the oil field below,

560
00:28:44,920 --> 00:28:48,900
it can be used as a hub for other oil fields nearby,

561
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:51,960
or it will be towed to another deep water location.

562
00:28:55,140 --> 00:28:58,300
There are more than 3,900 oil drilling platforms

563
00:28:58,400 --> 00:29:00,240
working in the Gulf of Mexico today.

564
00:29:03,580 --> 00:29:06,940
You don't care where oil and gas comes from.

565
00:29:06,941 --> 00:29:10,140
All you care about is that you can buy it cheaply.

566
00:29:10,240 --> 00:29:13,080
That pretty much sums up the attitude of a gas-guzzling

567
00:29:13,180 --> 00:29:17,320
world that's been on the move since the end of World War II.

568
00:29:17,420 --> 00:29:19,980
Consumption of oil in America tripled.

569
00:29:20,080 --> 00:29:23,460
In Western Europe, demand increased 15 times over.

570
00:29:23,560 --> 00:29:27,320
And in Japan, consumption increased 137 times.

571
00:29:31,560 --> 00:29:33,340
People in industrialized nations

572
00:29:33,440 --> 00:29:35,900
were enjoying an unparalleled standard of living

573
00:29:35,901 --> 00:29:38,800
in the early 70s when they were forced to confront

574
00:29:38,900 --> 00:29:41,800
the strategic value of oil.

575
00:29:41,900 --> 00:29:45,240
Attitudes were about to be tested for the first time

576
00:29:45,340 --> 00:29:47,880
as miles of motorists waited in their cars

577
00:29:47,980 --> 00:29:52,220
for higher priced gasoline in short supply.

578
00:29:52,320 --> 00:29:54,520
The rise of OPEC and the OPEC embargo

579
00:29:54,620 --> 00:29:59,400
in the winter of 73 and 74 gave the producing nations

580
00:29:59,500 --> 00:30:03,220
authority power to set the price and the level of production.

581
00:30:03,320 --> 00:30:05,600
And that was a dramatic change in the history

582
00:30:05,601 --> 00:30:06,600
the industry.

583
00:30:06,700 --> 00:30:08,800
With the price of the world's largest oil

584
00:30:08,900 --> 00:30:12,480
reserves controlled by OPEC, offshore oil companies

585
00:30:12,580 --> 00:30:14,940
began to beef up production.

586
00:30:15,040 --> 00:30:16,680
In an effort to remain competitive

587
00:30:16,780 --> 00:30:20,380
with the cheaply produced yet highly priced OPEC oil,

588
00:30:20,480 --> 00:30:22,680
they could afford to explore for new and bigger

589
00:30:22,780 --> 00:30:24,920
reserves of their own.

590
00:30:25,020 --> 00:30:29,600
That price spike gives the industry the wherewithal

591
00:30:29,700 --> 00:30:32,700
to look at dramatic new types of platforms,

592
00:30:32,701 --> 00:30:35,740
dramatic size of drilling equipment,

593
00:30:35,840 --> 00:30:37,340
all the things that it would have to do

594
00:30:37,440 --> 00:30:38,600
to conquer the North Sea.

595
00:30:41,340 --> 00:30:44,380
The North Sea is one of the most hostile environments

596
00:30:44,480 --> 00:30:48,280
on the planet, a place where 70-foot rogue waves

597
00:30:48,380 --> 00:30:50,720
are the rule, not the exception.

598
00:30:50,820 --> 00:30:53,320
It would prove to be the ultimate offshore testing

599
00:30:53,420 --> 00:30:55,820
ground.

600
00:30:55,920 --> 00:30:58,120
We have a little bit of a saying in the oil field

601
00:30:58,121 --> 00:31:03,120
is that if it looks to be a real desolate place

602
00:31:03,220 --> 00:31:05,460
or a harsh environment, that's probably a place

603
00:31:05,560 --> 00:31:08,000
that you're going to find oil or gas.

604
00:31:08,100 --> 00:31:10,460
In 1970, Phillips announced that they

605
00:31:10,560 --> 00:31:13,940
had discovered a giant oil field of more than 1 billion

606
00:31:14,040 --> 00:31:16,940
barrels in the Norwegian sector of the North Sea,

607
00:31:17,040 --> 00:31:20,440
enough to supply the entire world's energy needs for 20

608
00:31:20,540 --> 00:31:21,540
days.

609
00:31:21,640 --> 00:31:24,620
The Jacob rig Gulf tide was converted into a production

610
00:31:24,720 --> 00:31:27,640
platform and was soon producing 40,000 barrels

611
00:31:27,641 --> 00:31:31,240
from wells located 10,000 feet beneath the surface.

612
00:31:31,340 --> 00:31:34,040
Phillips finally had its feet planted firmly

613
00:31:34,140 --> 00:31:37,080
on the floor of the next frontier.

614
00:31:37,180 --> 00:31:40,980
What happened, we had the one successful discovery,

615
00:31:41,080 --> 00:31:43,760
which is what is now called EcoFist Field.

616
00:31:43,860 --> 00:31:47,220
But that gave them encouragement to continue drilling.

617
00:31:47,320 --> 00:31:49,400
And in fact, other fields were developed.

618
00:31:49,500 --> 00:31:53,960
So what started out as a very simple three or four platform

619
00:31:53,961 --> 00:31:58,000
concept quickly grew into what eventually turned out

620
00:31:58,100 --> 00:32:02,500
to be 25 plus platforms out in the immediate, what we call,

621
00:32:02,600 --> 00:32:04,000
greater Echo Fisk area.

622
00:32:04,100 --> 00:32:05,800
The weather conditions continued

623
00:32:05,900 --> 00:32:09,980
to haunt them as did the vastness of their discovery.

624
00:32:10,080 --> 00:32:14,180
On April 22, 1977, North Sea pioneers

625
00:32:14,280 --> 00:32:18,060
were hit with the unexpected when a well on the Echo Fisk Bravo

626
00:32:18,160 --> 00:32:20,060
platform blew out of control.

627
00:32:20,061 --> 00:32:23,360
100 men on board the platform jumped overboard

628
00:32:23,460 --> 00:32:26,360
into the icy waters after the oil ignited.

629
00:32:26,460 --> 00:32:30,300
The blowout spit an estimated 125 tons of oil

630
00:32:30,400 --> 00:32:33,300
into the air in the first 48 hours.

631
00:32:33,400 --> 00:32:35,540
A safety valve at the sea floor had not

632
00:32:35,640 --> 00:32:37,080
been installed correctly.

633
00:32:37,180 --> 00:32:39,920
The expression a little bit that I remember when I was there

634
00:32:40,020 --> 00:32:42,220
and some of the old guys was the only thing between you

635
00:32:42,320 --> 00:32:43,720
and hell is that valve.

636
00:32:43,820 --> 00:32:46,820
And that's exactly what the case is.

637
00:32:46,920 --> 00:32:49,320
The blowout lasted for eight days

638
00:32:49,321 --> 00:32:54,820
and tossed an estimated 22,500 tons of oil into the air.

639
00:32:54,920 --> 00:32:58,900
There were serious consequences, but luckily no loss of life.

640
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:02,260
Really one upshot was that the regulatory process

641
00:33:02,360 --> 00:33:05,900
tightened up dramatically after the incident.

642
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:07,800
Under strict new safety measures

643
00:33:07,900 --> 00:33:10,240
that included better firefighting procedures,

644
00:33:10,340 --> 00:33:13,220
the delicate balance between risk and reward

645
00:33:13,320 --> 00:33:15,580
continued on a grand scale.

646
00:33:15,680 --> 00:33:19,320
During the heyday, we had in excess of 1,000 people

647
00:33:19,321 --> 00:33:23,320
living and working offshore ecofist at any one point in time.

648
00:33:23,420 --> 00:33:25,320
So that gives you some kind of sense

649
00:33:25,420 --> 00:33:28,320
of the enormity of the operation.

650
00:33:28,420 --> 00:33:30,060
But there was a problem.

651
00:33:30,160 --> 00:33:36,580
By 1984, engineers realized that the platforms were sinking.

652
00:33:36,680 --> 00:33:40,880
There were such withdrawal rates of the oil and the gas

653
00:33:40,980 --> 00:33:44,080
and the water over time that eventually the rock

654
00:33:44,180 --> 00:33:46,080
that was 10,000 feet below the surface

655
00:33:46,081 --> 00:33:48,240
the surface started to lose strength

656
00:33:48,340 --> 00:33:51,020
and started to actually compact on itself,

657
00:33:51,120 --> 00:33:52,780
is the term we use.

658
00:33:52,880 --> 00:33:55,560
To fully imagine the magnitude of the problem,

659
00:33:55,660 --> 00:33:59,360
you must realize that the EcoFIS complex is one mile long

660
00:33:59,460 --> 00:34:04,740
from end to end, one mile worth of platforms, crew quarters,

661
00:34:04,840 --> 00:34:09,500
production equipment, separation facilities, and storage tanks,

662
00:34:09,600 --> 00:34:13,280
all connected by steel gangways with miles of pipe attached

663
00:34:13,281 --> 00:34:16,380
underneath, weighing more than 40,000 tons.

664
00:34:16,480 --> 00:34:19,860
And it was sinking, along with the illustrious history

665
00:34:19,960 --> 00:34:21,280
of offshore oil.

666
00:34:21,380 --> 00:34:25,020
Then out of the blue, somebody had a big idea.

667
00:34:25,120 --> 00:34:27,260
No one would have ever thought something like that

668
00:34:27,360 --> 00:34:28,460
was feasible.

669
00:34:28,560 --> 00:34:32,040
But like a lot of things, it started to build up steam.

670
00:34:32,140 --> 00:34:34,560
And what turned out to be really a harebrained idea

671
00:34:34,660 --> 00:34:37,480
in the beginning, people started to believe in.

672
00:34:37,580 --> 00:34:40,440
What oil workers, engineers, stockholders,

673
00:34:40,441 --> 00:34:42,980
and breath holders started to believe in

674
00:34:43,080 --> 00:34:46,920
was the idea to actually jack up six of the nine sinking

675
00:34:47,020 --> 00:34:50,820
platforms, jack them up 20 feet, four of them

676
00:34:50,920 --> 00:34:55,460
at the same time, jack them up just like you jack up a car,

677
00:34:55,560 --> 00:34:57,060
sort of.

678
00:34:57,160 --> 00:35:00,200
After extensive research indicated that further sinking

679
00:35:00,300 --> 00:35:04,200
was unlikely, the harebrained idea was about to come to life.

680
00:35:04,300 --> 00:35:06,860
In 1987, with the world watching,

681
00:35:06,861 --> 00:35:10,300
Several thousand workers temporarily installed 100

682
00:35:10,400 --> 00:35:15,000
hydraulic jacks with a lifting capacity of 700 tons each.

683
00:35:15,100 --> 00:35:18,000
With the weight of the platform supported by the jacks,

684
00:35:18,100 --> 00:35:21,740
welders cut out a three-foot section from each leg.

685
00:35:21,840 --> 00:35:25,980
Computers activated and monitored the actual elevation.

686
00:35:26,080 --> 00:35:29,580
Once the elevation was completed, longer leg sections

687
00:35:29,680 --> 00:35:34,480
were moved into place and bolted down.

688
00:35:34,481 --> 00:35:37,720
Amazingly, the harebrained idea worked.

689
00:35:37,820 --> 00:35:42,480
The $3.5 billion operation stands as a unique feat,

690
00:35:42,580 --> 00:35:45,860
never attempted before or since in the history

691
00:35:45,960 --> 00:35:47,520
of offshore drilling.

692
00:35:47,620 --> 00:35:50,160
And after all of this heavy lifting,

693
00:35:50,260 --> 00:35:54,100
the only serious accident was the loss of a fingertip and one

694
00:35:54,200 --> 00:35:54,960
toe.

695
00:35:55,060 --> 00:35:57,400
Since then, Ecovisc has been expanded

696
00:35:57,500 --> 00:36:00,040
with the construction of two new platforms

697
00:36:00,140 --> 00:36:01,980
and another 50-well unit.

698
00:36:01,981 --> 00:36:05,980
What we see now is the reality is that the oil that we thought

699
00:36:06,080 --> 00:36:09,320
would end maybe toward the turn of the century

700
00:36:09,420 --> 00:36:12,220
is now expected to continue for another 30 years.

701
00:36:12,320 --> 00:36:14,620
So it was really a remarkable feat.

702
00:36:14,720 --> 00:36:18,120
In 1979, 40 miles off the coast of Norway

703
00:36:18,220 --> 00:36:21,140
and 1,000 feet of water, Norsk Schell

704
00:36:21,240 --> 00:36:24,540
discovered an even larger reserve containing more than 46

705
00:36:24,640 --> 00:36:28,640
trillion cubic feet of gas, enough to supply 30 million

706
00:36:28,641 --> 00:36:30,880
with energy for 50 years.

707
00:36:30,980 --> 00:36:34,880
Retrieving it would revolutionize offshore production.

708
00:36:34,980 --> 00:36:38,480
Even before the gas field was considered commercially viable,

709
00:36:38,580 --> 00:36:41,880
Shell began building Troll, the world's tallest and heaviest

710
00:36:41,980 --> 00:36:45,260
platform in the calm, deep waters of Norway's fjords,

711
00:36:45,360 --> 00:36:49,000
safe from the force of nature's wrath in the North Sea.

712
00:36:49,100 --> 00:36:53,100
Reinforced with enough steel to build 15 Eiffel Towers,

713
00:36:53,200 --> 00:36:57,100
Troll's concrete base is as wide as three football fields

714
00:36:57,101 --> 00:37:00,840
fields and provide stability in the soft seabed.

715
00:37:00,940 --> 00:37:04,280
Ever so slowly, 10 of the most powerful tugs in the world

716
00:37:04,380 --> 00:37:06,600
began to tow the largest structure man has ever

717
00:37:06,700 --> 00:37:09,240
moved from the relative safety of the fjord

718
00:37:09,340 --> 00:37:12,080
out to the tumultuous North Sea.

719
00:37:12,180 --> 00:37:16,320
The 174 nautical mile journey would take six days

720
00:37:16,420 --> 00:37:20,820
and was choreographed by two satellite systems high above.

721
00:37:20,920 --> 00:37:23,560
Four days into the trip, ballast engineers

722
00:37:23,660 --> 00:37:25,760
flooded the huge chambers, beginning

723
00:37:25,761 --> 00:37:30,000
Troll's gradual descent in 1,000 feet of water.

724
00:37:30,100 --> 00:37:32,500
Tugs strain to hold Troll in position

725
00:37:32,600 --> 00:37:34,700
as it sunk to the bottom, permanently

726
00:37:34,800 --> 00:37:38,200
committed to its position on the Earth.

727
00:37:38,300 --> 00:37:42,980
In 1996, Troll began to bring up the equivalent of 600,000

728
00:37:43,080 --> 00:37:45,480
barrels of gas per day.

729
00:37:45,580 --> 00:37:50,120
At its current rate, Troll can fulfill 25% of Europe's gas

730
00:37:50,220 --> 00:37:51,820
needs till the year 2010.

731
00:37:55,760 --> 00:38:00,760
The oil platform troll is one of only a few man-made structures visible from space.

732
00:38:02,760 --> 00:38:09,760
Throughout its brief history, offshore oil has been driven as much by ingenuity, greed, and can-do attitude

733
00:38:09,761 --> 00:38:14,760
as it is by its need to supply the unrelenting demand for more and more oil.

734
00:38:14,761 --> 00:38:18,760
American motorists drive 2 trillion miles a year,

735
00:38:18,761 --> 00:38:25,760
the fact that shamelessly acknowledges that one of the real losers in the oil-fueled fight for the American dream is the environment.

736
00:38:25,761 --> 00:38:31,060
Santa Barbara is an interesting event in the offshore history.

737
00:38:31,160 --> 00:38:34,400
Certainly the most visible disaster

738
00:38:34,500 --> 00:38:36,880
in the history of offshore production.

739
00:38:36,980 --> 00:38:41,580
On January 28, 1969, a Union Oil Company platform

740
00:38:41,680 --> 00:38:44,420
six miles offshore was drilling almost a mile

741
00:38:44,520 --> 00:38:47,920
beneath the surface when it punctured a pocket of petroleum

742
00:38:48,020 --> 00:38:49,880
under high pressure.

743
00:38:49,980 --> 00:38:53,460
Oil exploded so violently it ruptured the seafloor

744
00:38:53,461 --> 00:38:57,840
the sea floor and gushed out of control.

745
00:38:57,841 --> 00:39:01,680
When the oil spill actually occurred in 1969,

746
00:39:01,681 --> 00:39:03,240
it was one of those pivotal events

747
00:39:03,241 --> 00:39:05,140
that just happened to be at the right time

748
00:39:05,141 --> 00:39:07,900
and the right place to not only cause this community

749
00:39:07,901 --> 00:39:10,360
grave concern, but became the catalyst for probably

750
00:39:10,361 --> 00:39:12,180
the national, if not international,

751
00:39:12,181 --> 00:39:13,440
environmental movement.

752
00:39:13,441 --> 00:39:15,260
It wasn't just seeing the beach blackened,

753
00:39:15,261 --> 00:39:18,440
it was the horror of seeing thousands of animals suddenly

754
00:39:18,441 --> 00:39:21,020
washing up covered with oil.

755
00:39:21,021 --> 00:39:23,920
And then the gruesome realization that this was not

756
00:39:24,020 --> 00:39:26,120
going to go away overnight.

757
00:39:26,220 --> 00:39:29,320
Not only was the oil not going to go away overnight,

758
00:39:29,420 --> 00:39:32,660
it was not going to stop for the next 10 days,

759
00:39:32,760 --> 00:39:36,140
dumping a total of 3 and 1 half million gallons of crude

760
00:39:36,240 --> 00:39:38,000
into the Pacific.

761
00:39:38,100 --> 00:39:40,940
The oil slick stretched for 20 miles down the coast

762
00:39:41,040 --> 00:39:43,000
and 40 miles out to sea.

763
00:39:43,100 --> 00:39:45,120
The California Department of Fish and Game

764
00:39:45,220 --> 00:39:47,740
reported the death of countless birds, fish,

765
00:39:47,840 --> 00:39:49,780
and other marine life.

766
00:39:49,781 --> 00:39:54,680
Cleanup required more than 1,000 people and 125 pieces

767
00:39:54,780 --> 00:39:58,220
of equipment to gather and vacuum up the oil.

768
00:39:58,320 --> 00:40:00,860
500 truckloads of oil-soaked straw

769
00:40:00,960 --> 00:40:03,260
were taken to three different landfills.

770
00:40:03,360 --> 00:40:06,400
There was no denying the televised images of an environment

771
00:40:06,500 --> 00:40:09,540
suffocated by offshore oil had been permanently seared

772
00:40:09,640 --> 00:40:12,200
into the conscience of an outraged citizenry

773
00:40:12,300 --> 00:40:14,900
and a casually regulated offshore industry.

774
00:40:15,000 --> 00:40:17,480
If you look at the history of offshore production

775
00:40:17,481 --> 00:40:19,580
from Santa Barbara toward the present,

776
00:40:19,680 --> 00:40:23,360
it's a fairly amazing record of learning how to be clean

777
00:40:23,460 --> 00:40:25,720
and doing it instead of talking about it.

778
00:40:25,820 --> 00:40:28,720
Offshore oil did indeed clean up its act.

779
00:40:28,820 --> 00:40:33,100
According to the US Minerals Management Service since 1985,

780
00:40:33,200 --> 00:40:36,700
534 platform and pipeline accidents

781
00:40:36,800 --> 00:40:40,400
have spilled 29,881 gallons of oil

782
00:40:40,500 --> 00:40:42,300
along the California coast.

783
00:40:42,400 --> 00:40:47,480
A fraction of the oil spilled in the 1969 disaster.

784
00:40:47,481 --> 00:40:51,180
In the 1960s, everything was moving very fast.

785
00:40:51,300 --> 00:40:53,220
There were no real safeguards.

786
00:40:53,560 --> 00:40:56,840
It was just move full speed ahead because we need to have the oil,

787
00:40:56,920 --> 00:40:58,260
we need to have the money that comes from it.

788
00:40:58,760 --> 00:41:01,960
Today, there's a much more guarded reaction,

789
00:41:02,420 --> 00:41:04,900
sort of a balanced view towards both making sure

790
00:41:04,901 --> 00:41:08,940
that if you're going to produce economic gain off of your natural resources,

791
00:41:08,941 --> 00:41:12,640
that you watch for and look at what the potential impacts are

792
00:41:12,641 --> 00:41:16,700
and that you weigh those long before you ever allow the development to start.

793
00:41:16,701 --> 00:41:19,200
The very nature of offshore oil production

794
00:41:19,300 --> 00:41:23,200
suggests that accidents will happen, and they do.

795
00:41:23,300 --> 00:41:25,440
Continued public and regulatory pressure

796
00:41:25,540 --> 00:41:27,340
have forced the offshore industry

797
00:41:27,440 --> 00:41:30,680
to develop more sophisticated ways of predicting trouble

798
00:41:30,780 --> 00:41:32,440
before it happens.

799
00:41:32,540 --> 00:41:34,580
High-tech solutions include everything

800
00:41:34,680 --> 00:41:38,020
from submersible cameras to ultrasound scanners

801
00:41:38,120 --> 00:41:39,760
that travel in pipelines.

802
00:41:39,860 --> 00:41:42,300
While there are no guarantees against future oil

803
00:41:42,400 --> 00:41:45,000
leaks, blowouts, and disasters, the offshore industry

804
00:41:45,001 --> 00:41:47,760
remains one of the most environmentally regulated

805
00:41:47,860 --> 00:41:49,240
throughout the world.

806
00:41:49,340 --> 00:41:54,440
The offshore industry's latest innovation is no exception.

807
00:41:54,540 --> 00:41:56,540
From the environmental standpoint,

808
00:41:56,640 --> 00:42:01,760
the vessel meets and exceeds all existing regulations

809
00:42:01,860 --> 00:42:03,260
to this day.

810
00:42:03,360 --> 00:42:06,660
You are looking at the future of offshore oil drilling.

811
00:42:06,760 --> 00:42:10,820
835 feet long, the discoverer Enterprise

812
00:42:10,920 --> 00:42:13,760
is only 50 feet shorter than the Titanic

813
00:42:13,761 --> 00:42:15,600
and is an amalgamation of everything

814
00:42:15,700 --> 00:42:18,860
the offshore industry has learned throughout its history.

815
00:42:18,960 --> 00:42:22,740
This is the next generation drill ship.

816
00:42:22,840 --> 00:42:27,840
Basically, the Enterprise is a one-stop ship

817
00:42:27,940 --> 00:42:29,280
for the industry.

818
00:42:29,380 --> 00:42:32,240
Instead of utilizing two or three vessels

819
00:42:32,340 --> 00:42:36,180
to accomplish a total well drilling and production

820
00:42:36,280 --> 00:42:40,260
and pipe laying package, the Enterprise offers everything

821
00:42:40,360 --> 00:42:41,520
that we need.

822
00:42:41,521 --> 00:42:45,820
Enterprise is capable of drilling in 10,000 feet of water.

823
00:42:45,920 --> 00:42:49,120
We have a total well depth of 35,000 feet.

824
00:42:49,220 --> 00:42:50,860
That's our limitation.

825
00:42:50,960 --> 00:42:52,960
If those are the drilling limitations,

826
00:42:53,060 --> 00:42:55,260
the expectations are even greater.

827
00:42:55,360 --> 00:42:59,080
Imagine trying to keep a vessel the length of the Astrodome

828
00:42:59,180 --> 00:43:03,000
over a 30-inch well on the ocean floor in rough weather

829
00:43:03,100 --> 00:43:04,680
without an anchor.

830
00:43:04,780 --> 00:43:07,220
These thrusters are 7,000 horsepower thrusters

831
00:43:07,320 --> 00:43:11,080
pushing 110,000 tons of vessel through the water,

832
00:43:11,081 --> 00:43:14,820
achieving zero drift when we do that.

833
00:43:14,920 --> 00:43:16,960
On the vessels from the past, you would achieve

834
00:43:17,060 --> 00:43:19,560
some type of drift off because there was not quite enough

835
00:43:19,660 --> 00:43:20,820
thrust to do that.

836
00:43:20,920 --> 00:43:23,600
But this vessel was designed with enough power

837
00:43:23,700 --> 00:43:27,260
to be able to rotate around that spot without any drift off.

838
00:43:27,360 --> 00:43:32,440
It can also store 125,000 barrels of oil.

839
00:43:32,540 --> 00:43:35,900
The world's energy needs have grown 1% to 2% a year

840
00:43:36,000 --> 00:43:37,900
over the last 15 years.

841
00:43:38,000 --> 00:43:40,380
And yet virtually all the new sources of oil

842
00:43:40,381 --> 00:43:44,280
are in geographically remote and politically risky areas.

843
00:43:44,380 --> 00:43:48,220
Just how deep are we willing to drill offshore for more oil?

844
00:43:48,320 --> 00:43:49,620
I don't think we know how far.

845
00:43:49,720 --> 00:43:53,720
The determining factor at the end of the day, in my view,

846
00:43:53,820 --> 00:43:55,460
will not be the technology.

847
00:43:55,560 --> 00:43:57,260
It'll be the economics.

848
00:43:57,360 --> 00:44:02,240
Can you justify from an economic standpoint to go out there?

849
00:44:02,340 --> 00:44:04,400
Because the fascinating part is, I

850
00:44:04,500 --> 00:44:07,640
think man will always figure out technically how to do it.

851
00:44:07,740 --> 00:44:09,680
You don't stop people from figuring out

852
00:44:09,681 --> 00:44:10,920
figuring out how to do it.

853
00:44:10,921 --> 00:44:12,620
It's just can you afford it?

854
00:44:12,621 --> 00:44:14,620
And I think that'll be the ultimate question.

